#### Article 25

Every Child who has excellence in addition to obtaining the rights as referred to in Article 10 through Article 23, has the right to receive special education.

# CHAPTER III

## OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

## Part One

Regency Government

#### Article 26

1. The Regency Government is obligated and responsible to:a. organize the Protection of Women and Children;  
b. undertake prevention efforts against acts of violence toward Women and Children;  
andc. guarantee the fulfillment of the rights of Women and Children as referred to in Article 5 through Article 25.  
2. The obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) may be implemented through:a. policies on the Protection of Women and Children from acts of Violence established by the central government;  
b. establishing programs and activities for the Protection of Women and Children from acts of Violence;  
c. conducting cooperation in the implementation of the Protection of Women and Children;  
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of the Protection of Women and Children;  
e. allocating budget for the implementation of the Protection of Women and Children in accordance with the financial capacity of the Regency;  
f. fostering and supervising the implementation of the Protection of Women and Children;  
g. establishing an integrated service center; andh. realizing a Child-Friendly Regency.  
3. The Regency Government as referred to in paragraph (1), establishes programs and action activities for the Protection of Women and Children in 1 (one) Regional Action Plan as the basis for the Agency to implement the Protection of Women and Children from acts of Violence.  
4. The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (3), constitutes part of the RPJMD.  
5. Provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (3) are regulated by Regent Regulation.

## Part Two Parents and Family

#### Article 27

Parents and Family are obligated and responsible to:
a. care for, maintain, and educate Children;  
b. develop Children in accordance with their abilities, talents, and interests;  
c. prevent marriage at Child age;  
d. provide character education and instillation of moral values in Children.  e. prevent violations of rights against Women and Children; andf. protect Women and Children from acts of violence.

## Part Three Community

#### Article 28

1. The Community is obligated and responsible to participate in organizing the Protection of Women and Children.  
2. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be implemented through:a. forming family partners at the sub-district and village level;  
b. forming Women and Children Protection units within community organizations;  
c. conducting socialization and education about the rights of Women and Children  independently;  
d. preventing violations of the rights of Women and Children;  
e. providing first aid to Women and Children who  become victims of acts of violence; andf. providing information or reporting to the competent authority if acts of Violence against Women and Children occur in their environment.  
3. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be implemented by individuals or groups, academics, Women and Children advocates, community-based integrated Child protection, community organizations/community institutions/ religious institutions/ other legitimate institutions.

# CHAPTER IV IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION

## Part One

General

#### Article 29

1. The Regency Government in organizing the Protection of Women and Children is coordinated by the Agency.  
2. The Regency Government in organizing the Protection of Women and Children as referred to in paragraph (1) may establish P2TP2A.  
3. Further provisions regarding P2TP2A are regulated by Regent Regulation.

#### Article 30

The implementation of the Protection of Women and Children is carried out through efforts to:
a. prevent;  
b. reduce vulnerability risks;  
c. handle;  
andd. rehabilitate.

## Part Two

Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence

#### Article 31

The implementation of the Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence is carried out through:
a. complaint, consultation, and counseling services;  
b. assistance services;  
c. medical rehabilitation services;  
d. social rehabilitation services;  
e. legal services; andf. social reintegration services.

#### Article 32

Complaint, consultation, and counseling services as referred to in Article 31 letter a include:
a. identification or initial recording of victims;  
b. case identification; andc. consent for action to be taken.

#### Article 33

Assistance services as referred to in Article 31 letter b include:
a. accompanying victims during the examination and health recovery process;  
b. accompanying victims during the examination process at the police, prosecutor's office and court;  
c. monitoring the interests and rights of victims in the examination process at the police, prosecutor's office and court;  
d. maintaining the privacy and confidentiality of victims from all parties who have no interest, including reporting by mass media;  
e. providing a sense of security to victims; andf. providing continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage.

#### Article 34

Medical rehabilitation services as referred to in Article 31 letter c include:
a. first aid to victims;  
b. treatment and recovery of physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of victims carried out by medical personnel and paramedics; andc. referral to health services.

#### Article 35

1. Social rehabilitation services as referred to in Article 31 letter d constitute services provided in order to restore the traumatic condition of victims.  
2. The form of services as referred to in paragraph (1) is through social support.  
3. Social support as referred to in paragraph (2) is carried out through:a. spiritual guidance to victims; andb. psychological recovery of victims.

#### Article 36

Legal services as referred to in Article 31 letter e are to assist victims in undergoing the judicial process by:
a. providing legal consultation which includes information regarding victims' rights and the judicial process;  
b. accompanying victims at the investigation, prosecution, and examination levels in court hearings and helping victims to fully describe the violence they experienced; andc. coordinating with fellow law enforcers, volunteer assistants, and social workers so that the judicial process proceeds as it should.

#### Article 37

1. Social reintegration services as referred to in Article 31 letter f aim to return victims to their families and social environment.  
2. Social reintegration services as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by the Regency Government in coordination with:a. relevant agencies and institutions both government and non-government; andb. community institutions and/or local community leaders.

## Part Three Child-Friendly Regency

#### Article 38

1. The Regency Government in order to realize a Child-Friendly Regency as referred to in Article 26 paragraph (2) letter h refers to the Child-Friendly Regency development policy that has been established.  
2. The Child-Friendly Regency development policy as referred to in paragraph (1) contains:a. the concept of Child-Friendly Regency;  
b. children's rights; andc. the approach to Child-Friendly Regency development.  
3. Further provisions regarding Child-Friendly Regency are regulated by Regent Regulation.
